{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Arise, go to Nineveh</b> — the royal city of Assyria, and today it is in ruins. And the sages of Israel in the land of Greece say that it is the one called Ortiḥeh [?], but I do not know. And now He did not command him to say, 'Yet forty days,' but only 'for their wickedness has come up before Me.' And the commentator who says that he feared he would be called a false prophet if HaShem relented from the evil — has not spoken correctly, for this reason He did not state to him until the second time, for there it is written: 'the proclamation that I speak to you,' and that was yet forty days. And furthermore: how would the prophet defy HaShem's mouth on account of his fear that the people of Nineveh would call him a false prophet — and what harm would that do him, for he does not dwell among them?! And furthermore: the people of Nineveh were not fools, for why would HaShem send a prophecy upon them except that they should return to HaShem, and if they do not return, the decree will be fulfilled upon them. And had they not known this truth — that if they return to HaShem, He will relent from the evil — then how would they call him a false prophet?! And the correct view is what our early sages, of blessed memory, said: that it displeased him that they should be delivered on account of Israel. Now, we find written: 'a great city to Elohim,' [meaning] that they feared HaShem from of old. And the commentator who says: [it means] it was a great city and precious in HaShem's eyes to destroy it — this is not the way of the language, for it is written: 'my soul shall be magnified in the eyes of HaShem' [1 Samuel 2:1], not 'to HaShem,' and here it is written 'to Elohim.' And it is written: 'all the nations are as nothing before Him' [Isaiah 40:17], and He does not care whether they are many. And the meaning of 'to Elohim' is that they feared HaShem in former days; only now, in the days of Jonah, they began to do evil. And were it not for this — that they were initially men of HaShem — He would not have sent His prophet to them. Now, we see that they repented a complete repentance, the like of which is not found, yet it is not written that they smashed the altars of Baal or hewed down idols. And from this we may learn that they were not idolaters."], :book :jonah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra writes אורטי\"ה and admits he does not know this identification; the identification with a Greek toponym (perhaps Ordysia or similar) is left untranslated as in the original. The phrase 'defy HaShem's mouth' renders מרה פי השם literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Jonah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=19&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:07:05.526311016Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}